WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.312
Collections and strings are where many
developers first feel Rust ownership in

2
00:00:04.392 --> 00:00:05.132
everyday work.

3
00:00:05.212 --> 00:00:06.919
`String` owns growable UTF-8 text.

4
00:00:06.999 --> 00:00:09.616
`&str` is a borrowed view of text owned
somewhere else.

5
00:00:09.696 --> 00:00:11.688
Choosing between them is not just syntax.

6
00:00:11.768 --> 00:00:15.409
It communicates whether a function needs
to own text or only read a view of it.

7
00:00:15.489 --> 00:00:17.196
`Vec<T>` owns a sequence of values.

8
00:00:17.276 --> 00:00:20.861
A slice borrows a view into an array,
vector, or string-like region of data.

9
00:00:20.941 --> 00:00:24.867
Passing a slice can be an excellent API
choice when a function only needs to inspect

10
00:00:24.947 --> 00:00:28.190
a range of values without taking ownership
of the whole collection.

11
00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:32.025
Passing an owned vector can be appropriate
when the function needs to consume,

12
00:00:32.105 --> 00:00:33.869
store, or transform the collection.

13
00:00:33.949 --> 00:00:35.713
Iteration also reflects ownership.

14
00:00:35.793 --> 00:00:39.150
Iterating by value consumes or moves
values when the type requires it.

15
00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:42.360
Iterating by shared reference reads values
without taking them.

16
00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:45.171
Iterating by mutable reference changes
values in place.

17
00:00:45.251 --> 00:00:49.063
The right choice depends on what the loop
is responsible for doing, not on habit.

18
00:00:49.143 --> 00:00:52.329
Unnecessary clones often appear when
ownership feels confusing.

19
00:00:52.409 --> 00:00:54.458
A clone can make the compiler error go
away,

20
00:00:54.538 --> 00:00:58.350
but it may also hide the fact that an API
is asking for ownership when it only needs

21
00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:00.934
a borrow, or that a scope is holding a
borrow too long.

22
00:01:01.014 --> 00:01:04.883
Reviewers should ask whether the clone
represents a real independent value or an

23
00:01:04.963 --> 00:01:06.101
avoidable workaround.

24
00:01:06.181 --> 00:01:08.001
At the same time, owned data is not bad.

25
00:01:08.081 --> 00:01:11.723
Sometimes returning or storing owned
values makes a design clearer, safer,

26
00:01:11.803 --> 00:01:15.672
and easier to maintain than threading
borrowed references through many layers.

27
00:01:15.752 --> 00:01:18.540
Practical Rust design balances efficiency
with clarity.

28
00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:22.660
The goal is intentional ownership, not the
fewest possible allocations at any cost.
